        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For a typical walking workout, start around 3 to 3.5 mph — brisk for most adults — and adjust until you are breathing noticeably harder but can still talk in sentences. Easy walking is usually 2.5 to 3 mph and fast walking 3.5 to 4.5 mph. Beginners and older adults may find 1.5 to 2.5 mph a better starting point.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the average walking speed on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              An ordinary adult walking pace is roughly 3 mph, or about 20 minutes per mile, with a wide spread around it. Younger and taller people often walk a little faster, and average walking speed tends to decline gradually with age. For a treadmill workout, the brisk range of about 3 to 3.5 mph is a typical target.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is 3 mph a good walking speed on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. For many adults 3 mph is a comfortable-to-brisk pace that counts as moderate exercise, especially with a modest incline of 2 to 5%. If 3 mph feels easy enough to chat freely, raise the speed slightly or add incline; if you cannot talk in sentences, slow down.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed is brisk walking on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Brisk walking is typically about 3 to 3.5 mph, though it depends on your height and fitness. The better definition is effort: brisk means your breathing is noticeably quicker and you can still talk in sentences, but would not want to sing. A cadence of roughly 100 steps per minute or more is a useful cross-check.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I walk on a treadmill to lose weight?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no special speed for weight loss. A brisk 3 to 3.5 mph with a modest incline is a good default because you can sustain it for 30 to 45 minutes several times a week, and consistency matters far more than speed. Diet has a bigger effect on weight than walking speed; ask a registered dietitian for nutrition advice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is a good treadmill walking speed for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often 1.5 to 2.5 mph to start, adjusted using the talk test rather than a chart. A moderate effort at 2.3 mph is a genuine workout. Clip on the safety key, change speed in small steps, use the handrails for balance if needed, and ask a doctor or physical therapist which intensity suits any health condition.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

